artifact.self_contained_problem:
Work with finite simple digraphs. For a digraph D, a proper k-dicolouring is a partition of V(D) into k parts each of which induces an acyclic subdigraph; the dichromatic number χ⃗(D) is the smallest such k. Two vertices u, v of a digraph are called twins if they are non-adjacent and, for every other vertex w, the arc relations between u and w match those between v and w (i.e. uw is an arc iff vw is an arc, and wu is an arc iff wv is an arc). Given two digraphs G₁ and G₂ and a set S of at most two pairwise non-adjacent vertices identified in both, the "glueing of G₁ and G₂ along S" is the digraph obtained by taking the disjoint union of G₁ and G₂ and identifying the vertices of S accordingly (with no new arcs added).

Define the class 𝒞 of digraphs recursively as the smallest class such that:
(i) the oriented K₂ (a single arc) belongs to 𝒞;
(ii) if G ∈ 𝒞 and v ∈ V(G), then the digraph obtained from G by adding a twin of v belongs to 𝒞;
(iii) if G₁, G₂ ∈ 𝒞 and S is a set of at most two pairwise non-adjacent vertices common to both, then the glueing of G₁ and G₂ along S belongs to 𝒞.

Question: Is there a constant K such that χ⃗(D) ≤ K for every D ∈ 𝒞, or does the dichromatic number of digraphs in 𝒞 take arbitrarily large values?

